cle disorders, where it exacerbates their condition. Additionally, it is common in patients with dementia, where the lack of appetite can contribute to malnutrition. The condition may be influenced by biological factors related to the underlying pathological process or treatment, as well as emotional factors that cause suffering.\n\nIn critically ill patients, anorexia can lead to inadequate nutrient intake, further complicating their health status. It can also be triggered by inflammation in patients with inflammatory bowel disease. Overall, anorexia is a multifaceted issue that necessitates careful management, including potential interventions such as enteral nutrition to address the nutritional deficits it creates.\",10\r\n3226,NUTRITIONAL INTERVENTION,\"Nutritional intervention encompasses a range of dietary strategies designed to meet the energy, fluid, vitamin, and mineral needs of various patient populations, particularly those who are vulnerable, such as the elderly and individuals with specific health conditions. It aims to improve health outcomes by addressing the nutritional status of patients, thereby slowing disease progression and preventing complications.\n\nIn the context of elderly patients, nutritional intervention focuses on preventing or managing sarcopenia and fragility, ensuring that their dietary intake supports overall health. For patients with liver disease, nutritional intervention is critical, involving the assessment and addressing of their dietary needs to enhance health outcomes. Similarly, for individuals with Anorexia Nervosa, the intervention emphasizes restoring healthy eating patterns and preventing refeeding syndrome.\n\nNutritional intervention is also vital for patients with cystic fibrosis, cancer, and those undergoing cancer treatment, as it involves personalized dietary strategies tailored to individual risk factors and health conditions. Overall, nutritional intervention plays a significant role in improving the nutritional 